{"id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:gradient-characterization","title":"prop:gradient-characterization","path":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.md","line":3882,"body":"","kind":"theorem","status":"not_started","tex":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex#prop:gradient-characterization","uses":["[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:gradient]]","[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:regular-set]]","[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#lem:gradient-characterization]]","[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#lem:transversal-representative-eq-gradient]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex","label":"prop:gradient-characterization","line":6638,"source":"\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:gradient-characterization}\\dcref{ch2:2-10}\n\n\n\\uses{def:gradient,def:regular-set,lem:gradient-characterization,lem:transversal-representative-eq-gradient}\nLet $(M,g)$ be Riemannian, $f\\in C^\\infty(M)$, and let $X$ be nowhere zero.\nThen\n\\[\nX=\\operatorname{grad}f\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad Xf=|X|_g^2\\text{ and }X\\perp\\text{ every regular level set of }f.\n\\]\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\begin{proof}\n\n\\sketch If $X=\\operatorname{grad}f$, the gradient characterization gives both conditions. Conversely, $df_p(X_p)=|X_p|^2>0$ makes every point regular; the orthogonality hypothesis and Lemma \\ref{lem:transversal-representative-eq-gradient} then give $X_p=\\operatorname{grad}f|_p$.\n\\end{proof}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Proposition</div></section><span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-70726f703a6772616469656e742d636861726163746572697a6174696f6e\"></span><span class=\"tex-source-references\" role=\"list\" style=\"display:block\"><span c